.TH SYSMON.CONF 5 "21 June 2000" "Version 0.83" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
sysmon.conf \- sysmond(man) configuration file
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I sysmon.conf
file is the main configuration file for the
.BR sysmond (man)
which monitors systems and services on various machines connected to a
network.

Lines starting with a hash mark (``#'') and empty lines are ignored.
Lines starting with a semicolon (``;'') are also treated as comments.

This release of
.B sysmond
is able to understand an extended syntax.  A rule may have a replacement
performed on it, if a variable is declared earlier in the configuration
file.

.SH ROOT
The ``root'' directive specifies the root object of the dependency tree.
All other objects should be reachable from this root through dependency
chains.  Objects not reachable from the root are logged as orphans.

Example:
.nf
  root = "my-gateway";
.fi

.SH GLOBAL SETTINGS
There are various global settings that
.BR sysmond
can have configured.  Currently there is some
inconsistency with some of the settings, but we expect to make
them more standard in a newer version of
.BR sysmond .

.SS Values that can be configured with ``set''

pmesg sender from subject upcolor downcolor recentcolor

These variables are configured in the following format

.IP
.nf
set pmesg = "value"
set sender = "value"
set from = "value"
set subject = "value"
set upcolor = "value"
set downcolor = "value"
set recentcolor = "value"
.fi
.LP

.SS Generic Variables
The set command allows the creation of generic variables which can
be automatically replaced further down in a configuration file.  This
can be used to seperate routers from servers, while still providing
an easy way to update all the devices being monitored with a new "contact".
The example that follows, all cases of $oncall will be replaced with
oncall@example.com

Example:
.IP
.nf
set oncall = "oncall@example.com"

router.example.com ping "main router" $oncall

.fi
.LP

.SS Values that can be configured with ``config''

queuetime include showupalso errorsto maxqueued replyto noheartbeat nologconnects
pageinterval logging dnslog dnsexpire statusfile statustempdir nosubject
numfailures minnumfailures flaptime html\ refresh date pidfile
snmp-trap authkey savestate cssfile

.SS queuetime
The ``config queuetime'' directive is used to specify the number of
seconds between the completion of the last test of the service
and the start of the next test of the service.  This time is in seconds,
and the default is 60.

.SS include
The ``include'' directive is used to specify the path to another
file that should be included at that point in the current file.  This
is useful if you have software that generates some of your configuration
files, and you wish to have a per-router configuration file, while
maintaining your current dependencies.  Other uses could be having an
oncall database that is generated to allow automatic changing of shifts.

Example:
.nf
  include "/etc/sysmon/routers.conf";
.fi

.SS showupalso
The ``config showupalso'' directive allows all hosts (both those as
being monitored as up and down) and services being monitored to appear in
the html or text status file that is created by sysmond.  This does
not affect the display of the curses client.

.SS errorsto
The ``config errorsto'' directive inserts an Errors-to: header in any
e-mail that sysmond generates to a contact.  This is useful if the
address that sysmond is sending from is not a real account, to allow
bounces or page-not-sent messages to be collected in a central location.

.SS maxqueued
The ``config maxqueued'' directive takes an integer argument that
specifies the number of simultaneous checks that may be performed at
once.  Large networks may experience problems with polling if sysmond
receives a large number of icmp responses at once because many hosts
are being pinged at once, or if the operating system or daemon has
a restricted number of filedescriptors available.  The default is 100.

.SS replyto
The ``config replyto'' directive inserts a Reply-to: header in any
e-mail that sysmond generates to a contact.  This is useful to have
users that reply to a message about an outage reach a support person
at an organization.

.SS noheartbeat
The ``config noheartbeat'' directive disables the default behavior of
sysmond to send a registration packet to our registration server.  This
information is collected to determine the hardware platforms and
operating systems being used for monitoring so we can concentrate
testing and development on these systems.  Disabling this feature will
cause no adverse affect to monitoring, as the packet is sent blindly
once on startup.

.SS nologconnects
The ``config nologconnects'' directive disables logging of client connections
to the sysmon daemon, reducing log verbosity in environments with
frequent client connections or monitoring systems that poll regularly.

.SS pageinterval
The ``config pageinterval'' directive allows you to configure a reminder
interval that a host is still down.  This is an integer representing
the number of minutes since the last e-mail message to a contact
about the outage.  Once that timer has been reached, another message is
sent.  The pageinterval can also be configured per-object (see OBJECT OPTIONS).

.SS logging
The ``config logging'' directive allows you to specify a syslog facility
to be logged to, or a file path.

.nf
  config logging syslog "daemon";
  config logging file "/var/log/sysmond.log";
.fi

Supported syslog facilities:
.IP
.nf
kern user mail daemon auth syslog lpr news uucp cron authpriv
local0-local7 none
.fi
.LP
The default is daemon.

.SS dnslog
The ``config dnslog'' directive allows you to configure the logging
interval of the internal dns cache statistics.  This number is
an integer representing the number of seconds between logging intervals.
The default is 900 seconds (15 minutes).

.SS dnsexpire
The ``config dnsexpire'' directive allows you to configure the internal
time-to-live of dns entries cached.  This number is an integer representing
the number of seconds an element should be allowed to stay in the cache.
The default is 1500 seconds (25 minutes).

.SS statusfile
The ``config statusfile'' directive takes two arguments.  The first is the
specification of the file, be it "text" or "html".  The second argument is
the path to the file that gets written.  The default is to not write a
status file.

.nf
  config statusfile html "/usr/local/htdocs/index.html";
.fi

.SS statustempdir
The ``config statustempdir'' directive specifies a directory where
sysmond should write temporary status files before renaming them to
their final location.  This is useful when sysmond has dropped
privileges, as it allows the daemon to write to a directory owned by the
unprivileged user.

.nf
  config statustempdir "/var/run/sysmon";
.fi

.SS cssfile
The ``config cssfile'' directive specifies a CSS file URL to use for
the HTML status page instead of the built-in inline styles.

.SS nosubject
The ``config nosubject'' directive specifies that there should be no
subject in the messages sent to the contact address.  This is necessary
to not consume characters for some e-mail to pager gateways.

.SS numfailures
The ``config numfailures'' directive takes an integer argument and
specifies the number of times that a site must be monitored as
down before a message is sent to the contact address.  The default is
4.

.SS minnumfailures
The ``config minnumfailures'' directive sets the minimum number of
consecutive failures before a host appears in the status file as
yellow/down.  Useful for filtering transient failures.  Default is 1.
Must be >= 1.

.SS flaptime
The ``config flaptime'' directive takes an integer in minutes.
After a host recovers, it continues to display as green for this many
minutes, preventing visual flapping.  Default is 0 (disabled).

.SS html refresh
Supply an integer argument in seconds (default 60) to put in the
Refresh pseudo-header of the HTML status page.

.SS date
Supply a string which may contain formatting codes from strftime(3)
(default "%x %X"), or the shorthand words "ISO" ("%F %T") or "DEC"
("%T %d-%b-%Y").

.SS pidfile
The ``config pidfile'' directive specifies the path to the PID file.

.SS snmp-trap
The ``config snmp-trap'' directive enables SNMP trap monitoring globally.
When enabled, sysmond opens a UDP socket on port 162 to receive SNMP
trap PDUs.  Individual objects can then use the trap_alert directive
to enable trap monitoring for specific hosts.

.SS authkey
The ``config authkey'' directive sets an authentication key required for
privileged client commands.  Clients must send "AUTH <key>" to authenticate
before performing certain operations.

.SS savestate
The ``config savestate'' directive specifies a file path where sysmond
will save the complete monitoring state in XML format on shutdown.

.SH OBJECT DEFINITIONS
Objects are defined using the following syntax:

.nf
  object name {
      ip "hostname-or-ip";
      type ping;
      desc "description";
      contact "email@example.com";
      dep "parent-object";
  };
.fi

The object name is used as a unique identifier in the dependency tree.
The ``ip'' field specifies the hostname or IP address to monitor.
The ``type'' field specifies the check type.  The ``desc'' field provides
a description shown in status output.  The ``contact'' field specifies
who to notify on failure.  The ``dep'' field creates a dependency on
another object (children are not checked when parent is down).

.SS Supported check types
ping, pingv6, tcp, udp, http, https, smtp, pop3, imap, nntp, ssh,
dns, snmp, sysmon, radius, ircd, umich_ldap

.SH OBJECT OPTIONS
The following directives are valid inside object definitions:

.SS ip
Hostname or IP address to monitor.

.SS type
Check type (see list above).

.SS port
TCP/UDP port number for tcp, udp, and protocol-specific checks.

.SS desc
Description string shown in status output and notifications.

.SS contact
E-mail address to notify on state changes.  Alternatively, the ``page''
directive can be used as a synonym.

.SS dep
Dependency on another object.  When the parent is down, this object
is not checked.  The ``child'' directive is the inverse (declares that
another object depends on this one).

.SS group
Group name for organizing objects in status displays.

.SS pause
Suspend monitoring of this object.

.SS reverse
Reverse logic: alert when the check succeeds instead of when it fails.

.SS queuetime
Per-object override of the global check interval in seconds.

.SS numfailures
Per-object override of the global numfailures threshold.

.SS pageinterval
Per-object override of the global re-page interval in minutes.

.SS contact_on
When to contact: "up", "down", or "both".

.SS spawn
Name of a spawn command to execute on state changes, referencing a
definition in the ``spawns'' block.

.SS pmesg
Per-object custom page message format string.  Supports the same
substitution variables as the global pmesg (set pmesg).

.SS trap_alert
Enable SNMP trap monitoring for this object.

.SS rtt_threshold
Round-trip time threshold in milliseconds.  If average RTT exceeds
this value, an alert is triggered.

.SS jitter_threshold
Jitter threshold in milliseconds.  If RTT variation exceeds this
value, an alert is triggered.

.SS SNMP Object Options

.SS community
SNMP community string for SNMP checks.

.SS oid
SNMP OID to query.

.SS snmp-type
Type of SNMP check: high, low, exact, rate, or uptime.

.SS snmp-high
Upper threshold for SNMP high/range checks.

.SS snmp-low
Lower threshold for SNMP low/range checks.

.SS snmp-exact
Exact value to match for SNMP exact checks.

.SS snmp-rate
Rate threshold for SNMP rate checks (per second).

.SS snmp-octets
Convert SNMP rate from bytes/sec to bits/sec (multiply by 8).

.SS snmp-oid-sec
Secondary OID for comparison monitoring.

.SS snmp-upmsg
Custom message when SNMP check passes.

.SS snmp-downmsg
Custom message when SNMP check fails.

.SS DNS Object Options

.SS dns-query
Hostname to query for DNS checks.

.SS dns-aa
Require authoritative answer flag in DNS response.

.SS dns-recursion
Enable recursive DNS queries.

.SS Authentication Options

.SS username
Username for authenticated protocol checks (POP3, IMAP).

.SS password
Password for authenticated protocol checks.

.SS secret
RADIUS shared secret.

.SS HTTP Object Options

.SS url
URL path to request for HTTP/HTTPS checks.

.SS urltext
Text string to search for in the HTTP response body.

.SS header
HTTP header name to check in the response.

.SS headerval
Expected value for the checked HTTP header.

.SH SPAWN DEFINITIONS
Named spawn commands can be defined in a spawns block:

.nf
  spawns {
    name "pagechris" {
      command "/usr/local/bin/page.sh %h %s %m";
    };
  };
.fi

Then referenced from objects with ``spawn "pagechris";''.

.SH PUSH NOTIFICATIONS (APNs / FCM)
The following directives enable mobile push notifications via the sysmon
web-ui backend.  The C daemon accepts and ignores these directives; they
are consumed by the Go web-ui backend which monitors host state changes
and sends push alerts to subscribed iOS and Android devices.
.TP
.B config push-notifications;
Enable push notification support.
.TP
.B config push-fcm-serverkey \fI"key"\fP;
Firebase Cloud Messaging server key for Android push notifications.
.TP
.B config push-apns-certfile \fI"path"\fP;
Path to the APNs TLS certificate in PEM format for iOS push notifications.
.TP
.B config push-apns-keyfile \fI"path"\fP;
Path to the APNs TLS private key in PEM format.
.TP
.B config push-apns-bundleid \fI"bundle-id"\fP;
The iOS app bundle identifier (e.g., com.example.sysmon).
.TP
.B config push-apns-production;
Use the production APNs gateway.  If omitted, the sandbox (development)
gateway is used.

.SH EXAMPLES

A minimal configuration:
.nf

  root = "gateway";

  config statusfile html "/var/www/html/sysmon.html";
  config logging file "/var/log/sysmond.log";
  config sender "sysmon";
  config dnsexpire 7200;

  object gateway {
      ip "10.1.1.1";
      type ping;
      desc "main router";
      contact "admin@example.com";
  };

  object webserver {
      ip "web.example.com";
      type ping;
      desc "web server";
      dep "gateway";
      contact "admin@example.com";
  };

  object webserver-http {
      ip "web.example.com";
      type http;
      url "/health";
      urltext "OK";
      dep "webserver";
      contact "admin@example.com";
  };
.fi

.SH FILES
.PD 0
.TP
.I /etc/sysmon.conf
Configuration file for
.B sysmond

